Built-in annotations.

An annotation is metadata you attach to code — a class, a method, a field, or a parameter — with an at-sign name. On its own, it doesn't change what the code does. It's a note that the compiler, or some tool, reads. And you've already used one all through module two: at-Override. This section rounds out the handful that the language and standard library ship, so the syntax stops looking like magic.

Start with the ones that talk to the compiler, which acts on them at build time. At-Override says "this method overrides a supertype method." It's not required, but it makes the compiler verify that you actually are overriding — so a mistyped signature, which would silently become a brand-new method, fails to compile instead. Put it on every override, always. At-Deprecated says "avoid this; it may be removed", and callers get a compiler warning. You pair it with a Javadoc note saying what to use instead, and you can add details, like since twenty-one, for-removal true. At-Suppress-Warnings says "I know about this warning, stay quiet here" — and you use it narrowly, on the smallest possible scope, only when you genuinely understand why the warning is safe to ignore. And at-Functional-Interface says "this interface has exactly one abstract method", which the compiler enforces, so the interface stays usable as a lambda target. That one is the subject of module six; it's mentioned here just so you recognise it. There's also a fifth, at-Safe-Varargs, which suppresses a specific generics-and-varargs warning.

So what is an annotation, actually? Annotations are declared with at-interface, and two meta-annotations define their reach. At-Target says where it may be placed — on a method, a field, a type, a parameter. And at-Retention says how long it survives. Source means it's discarded after compiling, like at-Override. Class means it's in the class file, but not loaded. And runtime means it's readable while the program is actually running.

That last one is the important bridge. A runtime annotation can be inspected as the program runs, using reflection — and that's the engine behind every framework you'll meet. At-Test, in JUnit. At-Entity, in J-P-A. At-Controller, in Spring. The framework scans your classes for its annotations, and wires up behaviour accordingly.

Annotations can also carry data, through named values in parentheses called elements. At-Test with a timeout of five hundred. At-Deprecated with since and for-removal. At-Suppress-Warnings with a list of warning names. And a single unnamed value can be written bare — at-Suppress-Warnings "unchecked" is just shorthand for value equals "unchecked".

So here's the takeaway. For your own everyday code, the one non-negotiable habit is at-Override on every override — it's free, and it catches real bugs. At-Deprecated and a tight at-Suppress-Warnings show up as you maintain code. And the deeper story — writing your own runtime annotations, and reading them with reflection to build framework-style behaviour — is exactly where module ten picks up. For now: an annotation is a labelled note on your code, and knowing target and retention tells you who gets to read it. That closes module three. You now have the full modern toolkit for modelling data; the coming modules turn to working with it.
